== 12. Wicked Company

The twelfth #emph[device] that Satan has to draw the soul to sin is to make it fond of wicked company and keep wicked society. And oh, the horrid impieties and wickedness that Satan has drawn men to sin by working on them to sit and associate themselves with vain persons!

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to dwell (till your hearts are moved) on those commands of God that expressly require us to shun the society of the wicked. Eph 5:11: “Have no fellowship#footnote[#emph[Non parentum aut majorum authoritas sed Dei docentis imperium] \[Not the authority of parents or forefathers, but the command of God who teaches\], Jerome. The commands of God must outweigh all the authority and example of men.] with the fruitless deeds of darkness, but rather expose them.” Prov 4:14, 15, 16: “Do not set foot on the path of the wicked or walk in the way of evildoers. Avoid it; do not travel on it. Turn from it and pass on by.” 1 Cor 5:9, 10, 11. 2 Thess 3:6. Prov 1:10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15. Turn to these Scriptures, and let your#super[+] souls dwell on them till a holy indignation is raised in your souls against fellowship with vain men. “God will not take the wicked by the hand,” as Job speaks; why then should you#super[+]? God’s commands are not like those that are easily reversed; they are like those of the Medes, which cannot be changed. If these commands are not kept by you now, they will at last be witnesses against you, and millstones to sink you, in the day when Christ judges you.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that their company is very infectious#footnote[Eusebius reports of John the Evangelist that he would not allow Cerinthus the heretic in the same bath with him, lest some judgment should fall on them both. Eusebius, book 3, chapter 25. A man who keeps bad company is like one who walks in the sun: he is tanned without noticing it.] and dangerous, as is clear from the Scriptures mentioned above. Ah, how many have lost their names, and lost their estates, and strength, and God, and heaven, and souls, by society with wicked men! As you#super[+] shun a stinking carcass, as the seaman shuns sands and rocks and shoals, as you shun those who have the plague-sore running on them, so you should shun the society of wicked men. As weeds endanger the corn, as bad humors endanger the blood, or as an infected house endangers the neighborhood, so does wicked company endanger the soul. Bias, a heathen, was at sea in a great storm, and saw many wicked men with him in the ship calling on the gods. “Oh,” he said, “stop praying, hold your#super[+] tongues! I would not have the gods take notice that you are here; they will surely drown us all if they do.” Ah, sirs! Could a heathen#footnote[Prov 13:20.] see so much danger in the society of wicked men, and can you#super[+] see none?

The third #emph[remedy] against this device#footnote[2 Tim 4:17. Isa 11:7. Ezek 29:3. Matt 10:16. Rev 9:3, 5, 10. Matt 3:7. Isa 10:17; 27:4; 55:13. Ezek 2:6. Judg 9:14. Job 21:18. Ps 18:42. Ps 1:4. Ps 18:42. Ezek 22:18, 19. Isa 65:5. Ezek 24:6. Lactantius says of Lucian (#emph[nec diis, nec hominibus pepercit]) that he spared neither God nor man. Such monsters are wicked men, which should make their company a burden and not a delight to all who have tasted the sweetness of divine love.] of Satan is always to look on wicked men under the names and descriptions by which the Scripture sets them out. The Scripture calls them #emph[lions] for their fierceness, and #emph[bears] for their cruelty, and #emph[dragons] for their hideousness, and #emph[dogs] for their filthiness, and #emph[wolves] for their cunning. The Scripture calls them #emph[scorpions, vipers, thorns, briers, thistles, brambles, stubble, dirt, chaff, dust, dross, smoke, scum,] as you may see in the margin. It is not safe to look on wicked men under the names and descriptions they give themselves, or that flatterers give them; this may delude the soul. But looking on them under the names and descriptions the Scripture gives them may keep the soul from frequenting their company and delighting in their society. Do not tell me what this man calls them, or how such and such a person regards them; tell me what the Scripture calls them. How does the Scripture regard them? As Nabal’s name was, so was his nature; and as wicked men’s names are, so are their natures. You may know well enough what is within them by the fitting names the Holy Spirit has given them.

The fourth and last #emph[remedy] against#footnote[“O Lord, do not let me go to hell, where the wicked are, for, Lord, you know I never loved their company here,” said a gracious gentlewoman when she was about to die, being in much trouble of conscience.] this device of Satan is to consider that the society and company of wicked men have been a great grief and burden to those precious souls who were once glorious on earth and are now triumphing in heaven. Ps 120:5, 6: “Woe to me that I dwell in Meshech, that I live among the tents of Kedar! Too long have I dwelt among those who hate peace.” So Jeremiah: “If only I had a traveler’s lodge in the wilderness, I would abandon my people and depart from them, for they are all adulterers, a crowd of faithless people” (Jer 9:2). So they tormented Lot’s#footnote[2 Pet 2:7, 8. See Beza.] “righteous soul” by their “depraved conduct.” They made his life a burden; they made death more desirable to him than life; indeed, they made his life a lingering death. Guilt or grief is all the good that gracious souls get by keeping company with wicked men.
